Z||000009||DaRkLoRD||12-19-2001||09:04 PM||||it's my understanding that the new CDs with the copy protection can't be played on most of the CD platforms in use now, including all the new game consoles, and MACS!!<P>maybe I'm just weird for seeing a potential problem with that... I have my stereo in my bedroom, but I listen to CDs while I work.. now if I can't play CDs on my computer while I work, I might as well stop buying them.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||4
Z||000010||Cliff Pennock||12-19-2001||09:47 PM||||There <B>is</B> a problem with this new copy protection. First of all, these CDs are unmarked as having this protection so there's no way of knowing in advance if you buy one or not. Second, they will not play on many PC-CD players and most importantly <B>they will not play on many CAR-CD players</B>. That sucks bigtime. I never listen to CDs at home but I <B>always</B> listen to CDs in my car.<P>I don't care if the CD can be copied or not (in fact, they <B>can</B> be copied quite easily) but I hate the fact that I can't play them anywhere I like.<P>I said it before in another thread: if the record companies would stop making these rediculous profits on CDs, maybe more people would buy them and less people would copy them. The artist only gets a few dimes per CD, the shop gets a few dollars, and the rest is for the record company.||||reg||1
