Z||000017||artherd||12-20-2001||05:22 AM||||Um. I NEVER have orgional CDs in my car.  EVER.<P>They're all CD-R dupes of the orgionals I keep at home.<P>We have a legally defined RIGHT to make such copies, it's covered under "BACKUP COPIES" section of copyright and music licence law.<P>This is the problem people.  first it's no backup copies for the car (Psst: just buy two full licences from us, no problem guy!) then next it's pay-per-play only. (Psst: our cds were being copied, so we lost all of our money overnight, so now we're going to start selling bottled air to you morons, and you're gonna like it)<P>Can you tell I like record companies?<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000018||OcalaFiero||12-20-2001||09:53 AM||||With all of the new schemes for copy protection and "pay per play" it reminds me of that now (thankfully) failed DVD format DiVX of a few years back. If you're not familiar with it, it was heralded as the simple way to "buy" dvd's. You'd go to a DiVX retailer (I think that only Circuit City bought into this fiasco) and buy a DiVX format DVD player, and install it, making sure that it was connected to a phone line (this is where the fun part starts). When ever you "bought" a DiVX DVD at the store (Circuit City) you paid only about 4.00 and took that low priced DVD home. When you put it into the player, it dialed in, verified the serial number of the DVD and presto you had your brand new copy of whatever movie you had just "purchased"...but only for 3 days or so, when the "license" expired. But what if you wanted to keep the movie forever? well, just select the purchase option on the movie menu and for another 20.00 or so it was yours. <P>Give the music companies a chance, this type of scheme will look appealing to them, as they can then counter the argument that people use napster type services to see if they like the music or not. This way the music companies can grant you a license to "rent" a cd before you "buy" it. what a mess, I guess that after the few dollars per CD that go to production and the artists, all of the leftover "marketing money" is really important to the record companies.<P>Mike<BR>Ocala, FL||||reg||1
Z||000019||isthiswhereiputausername?||12-20-2001||10:18 AM||||Dont worry about it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">    Takes companies months to produce some kind of protection and group crackers 5 minutes to break. <P>these protected audio cds were released on the scene within 1 HOUR of being publically released..  <P>PS.. the way they did it?   digital stereo hooked up to a high quality audio card and ripped it using their audio card.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||RedHaze86SD4||12-20-2001||10:24 AM||||whats messed up is the fact that Sony is one of the leading developers for MP3 technology and yet theyre also one of the labels thats so down on the sharing of MP3s theyre out to make a buck so they think we should pay for each mp3 we get. <BR>As far the MP3 changers: Sony has 5 due to hit shelves in the first half of 2002. hope they actually do and don't "tied" up yet again. <BR>Also, Sony is developing headunits for the new "SD" format which is replacing the CD format. This is being introduced around August of 2002 I think. Well, they were supposed to have the press release September 15th (my bday  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) of this year but it got delayed untill next. the new SD format can hold 1.2 gigs on a single cd   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> And they have DVD quality or better sound when used with Dolby surround. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000021||Ken Wittlief||12-20-2001||10:29 AM||||anything that you can get to come out the speakers of your stero, you can record on a cd player - all you need to do is take the audio patch cord out of the CD player and put it into the IN connector on a PC - then record the sound as wave files, and burn a new CD<P> yes you have gone digital &gt; analog &gt; digital and lost some sound quality - but I doubt if its noticable, esp in a car<P> and you cant dupe the first one at 16x, but if you really want a copy its not all that hard.<P> When I was a teenager we use to copy vinyl records onto cassette tape by putting MICROPHONES by the speakers of the sterio - early casstte recorders and record players did NOT have line in and line out jacks.<P> Boy, those were the days!||||reg||10
Z||000022||Cliff Pennock||12-20-2001||10:40 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>yes you have gone digital &gt; analog &gt; digital and lost some sound quality</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, you can hook up the digital output of your CD player to the digital input of your soundcard. There. Perfect copies.||||reg||1
Z||000023||MinnGreenGT||12-20-2001||11:13 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RedHaze86SD4:<BR><B>Also, Sony is developing headunits for the new "SD" format which is replacing the CD format. This is being introduced around August of 2002 I think. Well, they were supposed to have the press release September 15th (my bday   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) of this year but it got delayed untill next. the new SD format can hold 1.2 gigs on a single cd    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> And they have DVD quality or better sound when used with Dolby surround. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually there are now two superior audio formats.  Sony's SACD (Super Audio Compact Disc) and Phillips(?) DVD-Audio.  Both are similiar in content and both have phenominal sound quailty.  But there will be a battle (like VHS vs. Beta) for awhile before a clear format rules.<P>As to the DVD vs. DIVX- that wasn't so bad.  I bought a DVD/DIVX player (right before the program ended- CC gave me $100 cash back when the program flopped).  Anyway- It was basically like renting a movie.  I could either go to Circuit City (or Audio King) and buy a DIVX disc, or go to Blockbuster and rent it for the same price.  And yes, you could buy the "platinum" DIVX if you really liked it and end up with the same final cost as a DVD.  Irreguardless, the program died, and I benefitted ($100+ DIVX movies for $.99!).<P>As to the CD copy protection thing... I'm all for copy-protection.  As long as it doesn't interfere with the playability of the disc (like it is).  I used to rent VHS and copy them to watch later.  Since I changed my views (DVD rules) I have not copied a single disc.  If I really feel that a movie is good enough to watch more than once or twice- then it's worthy of my investment.<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000024||MinnGreenGT||12-20-2001||11:46 AM||||One more thing to add... basic info can be found at their website: <A HREF="http://www.musichelponline.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.musichelponline.com/</A>  including an email address to send comments/questions to:  <A HREF="mailto:umg@umusic.com">umg@umusic.com</A> <P>I just sent them the following message, and I encourage you to send something as well (and although we are a small community every message counts).<BR>------------------------------------------<BR>to: <A HREF="mailto:umg@umusic.com">umg@umusic.com</A> <P>UMG Music-<P>It has been brought to my attention that you have begun producing a<BR>copy-protected CD (Fast & Furious Sndtrk-2001).  I feel that copy-protection<BR>is an important thing in today's high-tech society.  But I feel that<BR>compromising the playability of a CD for the sake of copy protection is<BR>wrong.  If I decide to spend my hard-earned money on a CD, I expect to be<BR>able to play it wherever I like! In my car (I understand there are some Car<BR>CD players that are also incompatible), especially in my computer at my desk<BR>(I am a graphic designer, and my primary computers are Macintosh systems).<P>I would offer the suggestion that you suspend your "test" CD program until<BR>you have a system that will work in every CD player, computer, car audio<BR>player, gamestation, and the like! You really should think about your<BR>consumers before yourselves.  Ever recall the old adage: "The customer is<BR>always right"? <P>Maybe you ought to step back and "look" before you "do" something like this.<P>Thank you for your time.<BR>Rob Bartlett<BR>Xtra Lite Display Systems, Inc.<BR>Burnsville, MN<BR>------------------------------------------||||reg||1
Z||000025||isthiswhereiputausername?||12-20-2001||12:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff Pennock:<BR><B> Actually, you can hook up the digital output of your CD player to the digital input of your soundcard. There. Perfect copies.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yep.. Just make sure you use a Digital audio cable and not a cheap analog stereo cable.<P><BR>||||reg||1
