Z||000031||84fierotrevor||12-20-2001||04:47 PM||||If u want to play them in you'r car,<BR>Go to gamecopyworld.com download the patch upload the music to ur pc. then burn it with the image file.<BR>store ur purchesed cd at home and use ur copyed one in ur car. If it get's scratched up then u still got you'r backup.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000032||theogre||12-20-2001||05:08 PM||||As much as people hate how the record compnaies do bussiness.... They own the material. If you don't like what they do with it then don't buy it. I buy very little music. There's bloddy little worth buying. I'm not paying $10-20 for a CD with one good song. I might buy it if I see it in a $3-5 bargain bin in a few months...<P>The big push for copy protection is a direct result of people on Napster and related systems that commonly copy titles by the thousands. They didn't really care about people who made tapes of CDs to play in their cars and that sort of thing. Don't get me wrong, that annoyed them but by comparison Napster was like kicking them in the teeth. It's the differance between tip toeing past a dragon and hitting it with a club.<P>The music industry is going thru the same trial period with copy protection that software went thru a number of years ago. They'll eventually figure out something. It's very unlikely they will eliminate it even if they have to invent other media formats. <P>The artists are as much to blame for the money, or lack of it, they are paid as anything else. A vast majority of them are making records under the "work for hire" provisions of U.S. copyright law. look it up. ( <A HREF="http://www.loc.gov" TARGET=_blank>www.loc.gov</A>  )<P>The good thing about the internet is that artists are no longer bound to the record labels for distribution of material. The problem is the artists still need a way to get paid for stuff. Many of them will use whatever copy protection becomes the standard.<P>Under traditional copryright law, you have "fair use" rules and rights to archival storage. But enter the Milenium Digital Copyright Act, which has effectivly overridden many of the rights you had for making archive copies of things. Once a product is using some type of copy protection your rights are limited to whatever the publisher gives you. They can even set expiration dates in many of these protection systems. Go to Microsofts site windowsmedia.microsoft.com and look at the developer materials. The WMA formats already include the ability to do pay per view/play.<P>What? You want to make a backup of that Ebook? It's Copy Protected? Guess what.... It's now illegal to write use or posess software that cracks that copy protection so you can back it up etc. (As one case Adobe initiated against a Russian programer vividly pointed out... )<P>Does any of the stuff they are doing now eliminate copies? no. That's part of why they pushed so hard for the MDCA. (OR is it DMCA, never can keep it straight) They want, and got, a law that allows them to procecute the author of a piece of "cracking" software <u>even if the software is only used for legitimate archiving of material.</u><P>can the protection used by Universal and others be cracked? Undoubtedly, but any person writing using or distributing software or hardware to do so is now subject to Federal prosecution. (You can look up the stuff about the Russian guy arrested in California at <A HREF="http://www.news.com" TARGET=_blank>www.news.com</A>  He was arrested for being one of the authors that broke Adobe's Ebook file format and let you save books as regular PDF files.)<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||9
Z||000033||btoth||12-20-2001||07:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by The Fieromaster:<BR><B> I noticed that SOME copying software copies ANYTHING and others dont!! I think its the same situation you mentioned above!  I am thinking that i will stick with my old ass burner so i can copy anything   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not all copy protection is the same. <P>How most work (from what I understand) is that the copy protection is written outside the normal track(s) that contain the actual data.<P>Most new CD-ROMs and CD-Writers can read most if not all of these sectors.  The program (a game for instance) when run, will look for those sectors on the CD and if they are there, it knows that the proper CD is in the drive.<P>Most CD-burners cannot, however, write those sectors to a CD-R because they either have built in error correction or simply can't record that way.  Most CD writing software is that way too, it'll detect the error and "fix" it.<P>However, using a program like Blind Write and Blind Read, and a CD-Writer that can read and write all sublayers (what they call it) of a CD then it's very easy to do.<P>But usually, this is only nessessary for software, but should work with any copy-protected music as well.  <P>I've actually found myself using copies of my own CDs in my car because I think that it's the stupid CD-player sucking in my CDs that eventually lead to the unplayable last tracks on the CDs from scratching the outer edge of the CD while pulling it in.<P>(BTW, if anyone has a more technical way to explain the copy protection stuff that I mentioned above, please do.)||||reg||1
